      {
        "id": "s3_n2_bond_order",
        "kind": "equation",
        "label": "N₂ bond order and magnetism",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "N₂ result",
        "lines": [
          "Bond order = ½ (Nb − Na), where Nb and Na",
          "are the electrons in bonding and",
          "antibonding molecular orbitals.",
          {
            "text": "Bond order of N₂ = ½ (10 − 4) = 3   (N≡N)",
            "style": "eq"
          },
          "N₂ is diamagnetic — it has no unpaired",
          "electrons."
        ],
        "why": "Ten electrons sit in bonding MOs and four in antibonding, so the net bonding is three pairs — the triple bond. With every electron paired, the molecule is repelled weakly by a magnet: diamagnetic.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Counting σ1s² and σ*1s² on one side only. Both are counted: σ1s in Nb, σ*1s in Na.",
          "Writing paramagnetic. N₂ has zero unpaired electrons."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "10 bonding, 4 antibonding: half of six is three. All paired means diamagnetic.",
        "margin_note": "Always define Nb and Na once before using the formula."
      },
      {
        "id": "s4_o2_config",
        "kind": "equation",
        "label": "O₂ configuration",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "O₂ config",
        "lines": [
          "O (Z = 8): 1s² 2s² 2px² 2py¹ 2pz¹",
          "O₂ has 16 electrons. In O₂ the σ2px level",
          "lies BELOW the π2p levels. The",
          "configuration is:",
          {
            "text": "σ1s² σ*1s² σ2s² σ*2s² σ2px² π2py² π2pz² π*2py¹ π*2pz¹",
            "style": "eq"
          },
          "By Hund's rule the last two electrons",
          "occupy π*2py and π*2pz singly."
        ],
        "why": "O₂ uses the normal order (σ2px first), and its last two electrons land one each in the two degenerate π* orbitals — the single fact that makes O₂ paramagnetic.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Pairing the last two electrons in one π* orbital. Hund's rule puts one in each.",
          "Carrying the N₂ order into O₂. For O₂, σ2px fills before the π2p pair."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Sixteen electrons: σ first in the 2p block, and the last two sit apart, one in each π* orbital.",
        "margin_note": "The two superscript 1s at the end are the answer's key detail."
